 "rulebook": "ITTF Handbook 2024, The Laws of Table Tennis, for the equipment: 2.3 (the ball), 2.4 (the racket). Bouncing the ball on the racket is a ball-control drill, not an event with a rule book: the number of consecutive bounces asked for is this benchmark's line",
 "deviations": [
  "2.4 (the racket): the blade is a rigid 15 cm square, 1.5 cm thick, 0.14 kg with its handle (legal: the Laws set no size, shape or weight); its covering is one contact law on both faces (RoboOlympics' ball-paddle pair, hand-picked, not identified), and it is fixed to the right hand, because the robot's fist cannot hold a handle",
  "the start: nobody hands the robot a ball; it hangs at rest in front of the robot and is let go at 2.0 s",
  "a bounce is a hit: so that the rebounds of one strong hit off a still blade are not counted, the ball must leave a counted bounce with at least 0.98 of the speed it reached the blade with (both taken at the height of the touch; a still blade returns about 0.95); a bounce that does not is not counted, and is not a fault"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Keep the table tennis ball that is dropped in front of you bouncing on the paddle in your right hand: 20 bounces in a row, each back up to the height the ball was dropped from.",
  "rules": "A table tennis racket (a rigid square blade 0.15 m wide and 0.015 m thick on a short handle, 0.14 kg) is fixed to your right hand. The official ball (40 mm, 2.7 g; it flies in air, with drag) hangs at rest, its centre 1.2 m above the floor, until 2.0 s after the start, and then falls. You succeed after 20 bounces in a row on the blade (either face; the faces need not alternate). A bounce is a touch of the blade after which, before the ball touches the blade again, the ball's centre rises at least 0.15 m above the height at which it left the blade and reaches at least the height it was dropped from, 1.2 m above the floor, and which the ball leaves with at least 0.98 of the speed it reached the blade with (both speeds taken at the height where the touch began). On a still blade every bounce is lower than the one before (the ball keeps about nine tenths of its height, 0.95 of its speed), so each bounce needs a hit of the blade; a rebound that rises enough but was not hit is not counted, and is not a fault. A touch is all contact of the ball with the blade from the first contact on, with breaks shorter than 0.02 s; a bounce's own contact lasts about a millisecond. You fail if the ball comes back to the blade after a touch without having risen that much; if a touch lasts longer than 0.02 s from its first contact to its last (the ball rests, rolls or is carried on the blade); if the ball touches the floor or anything of yours other than the blade (the racket's handle, the hand and the arm included); if you touch the ball before it is dropped; if you fall; or after 20 s. A fall is your pelvis lower than 0.35 m above the floor, or any part of you other than your feet touching the floor (the racket on the floor is a fall).",
  "venue": "You stand on a level floor facing +x, the paddle hanging from your right hand, its handle in the fist and the blade beyond it (the blade's centre 0.175 m from the wrist). The ball hangs 0.36 m ahead of your pelvis and -0.2 m to your left (negative: to your right), its centre 1.2 m above the floor, and is let go, with no speed and no spin, 2.0 s after the start. There is no wind.",
  "difficulty": "Every bounce needs an upward hit of the right strength at the right instant, with the blade level and under a 40 mm ball: a tilt of a degree sends the ball off the 15 cm blade within a few bounces, and the arm that holds the blade gives way a little at every hit. It has to go right twenty times in a row."
 },
